<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Naval Defence Act 1889</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1889">1889</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Naval Defence Act 1889 was a British law that formally adopted the two-power standard, under which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> was to be at least as strong as the next two largest navies combined, then those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. It provided about 21.5 million pounds for the construction of 70 new warships, including ten battleships, and began a sustained expansion of the fleet that lasted until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p></div>
